 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training typically takes 15–30 minutes for standard scenes (e.g., Mip-NeRF 360) on a modern GPU [S7, unofficial]. The loop is dominated by rasterization and backward pass, which are $O(N \cdot P)$ where $N$ is the number of Gaussians and $P$ is the number of pixels [S4].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** VRAM usage scales with the number of Gaussians and image resolution. Standard implementations store the entire training dataset in VRAM, which limits scalability to large scenes [S11, unofficial][S4]. Optimizations like CLM and Grendel-GS address this by offloading or distributing data [S2, S4].
-*   **Performance:** 3DGS training is significantly faster than NeRF training (which can take hours) while achieving comparable or superior image quality (PSNR/SSIM) [S2][S7, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Real-time rendering capability post-training, fast convergence, high-fidelity details [S7, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** High VRAM consumption for large scenes, potential for "floaters" or over-reconstruction if densification is not properly controlled [S2, S3].
-*   **When to use:** When real-time rendering is required and training time is a constraint.
-*   **When not to use:** When VRAM is extremely limited and no offloading strategies are employed, or when implicit representations are preferred for compactness.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Training typically requires 30,000 iterations [S1][S5, unofficial]. Each iteration involves rasterization and backpropagation, which are highly parallelizable on GPUs [S4].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage scales with the number of Gaussians $M$. Standard implementations store the entire training dataset in VRAM for speed, which can exceed GPU capacity for large scenes [S11, unofficial][S4].
+*   **Performance:** 3DGS training is significantly faster than NeRF-based methods, often completing in minutes to hours depending on scene size, compared to hours or days for NeRFs [S2][S7, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Real-time rendering capability after training, high visual fidelity, and explicit representation allows for easy editing and integration with other graphics pipelines [S2][S7, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** High VRAM consumption for large scenes, potential for "floaters" or artifacts in unobserved regions, and sensitivity to initialization and hyperparameters [S2][S11, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When real-time rendering of high-quality static or dynamic scenes is required, and sufficient GPU memory is available.
+*   **When not to use:** For scenes with extreme dynamic range or complex lighting that cannot be captured by spherical harmonics, or when VRAM is severely constrained without offloading techniques [S2][S11, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [S3]. The method builds on the concept of radiance fields but replaces neural networks with explicit 3D Gaussians [S7, unofficial]. It was designed to overcome the slow training and rendering speeds of NeRFs [S2]. The original implementation initializes Gaussians using sparse point clouds from SfM tools like COLMAP [S3]. The training pipeline involves differentiable rasterization, allowing end-to-end optimization of Gaussian parameters via gradient descent [S4].
+3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [S3]. It builds on the concept of radiance fields but replaces neural networks with explicit 3D Gaussians [S7, unofficial]. The method was designed to address the slow training and rendering speeds of NeRFs while maintaining high visual quality [S2]. The original implementation initializes Gaussians using sparse point clouds from Structure-from-Motion (SfM) tools like COLMAP [S3]. The training pipeline leverages differentiable rasterization to optimize Gaussian parameters end-to-end [S4].
 
